  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.5 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so without noise steps overshoot along x above learning rate 0.4 and grow without bound above 0.8.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the next mini-batch step, the way the ball moves. Grey arrow: the full-data step (learning rate × true slope, pointing downhill). Both are drawn at 2× the step length.",
    "Noise values come from a fixed seed. New run continues the sequence, so its noise differs. Reset replays the sequence from the start and clears the ghost path.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn square. Off-surface positions are capped on screen, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, a single step can raise it.",
    "When running, the ball takes five steps per second at 1× speed. Changing a setting mid-run affects only the steps that come after."
  ]
}